<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    "Pin" - the original Nudge look: soft-UI / neumorphic. Pillowy corners, every raised control
    defined by a shadow rather than an outline, and library tiles carrying a genuine two-layer
    emboss (a dark shadow down-right paired with a light highlight up-left).

    This dictionary is deliberately EMPTY. Pin's values are the base values already defined in
    Layout.xaml and Effects.xaml, so there is nothing here to override - restating them would just
    create a second copy to keep in sync, and they would silently drift apart the first time one was
    edited without the other.

    It still exists as a real file, rather than the style overlay simply being absent for Pin,
    because UiStyleService swaps this slot in Application.Resources.MergedDictionaries by index: it
    needs a Pin entry to swap *back to* when someone returns from another style, and an entry it can
    find in order to know which slot the overlay occupies at all. See Style.Crisp.xaml for what an
    overlay that actually changes something looks like.
  -->

</ResourceDictionary>
